% ============================================================================
% energy-form-family — each named form of energy and which of the source's
% two families it belongs to: POTENTIAL or KINETIC (adj-facts-stdlib,
% PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `energy-forms.adj` (`energy_form_token(form,
% token)`, ONE defining token per energy form: chemical → bonds, thermal →
% heat, etc.). This table recalls a DIFFERENT axis the same EIA source states
% about the same eight forms: which of the two named FAMILIES each one
% belongs to. `energy-forms.adj`'s own header already notes "EIA groups these
% forms into two families — POTENTIAL energy (chemical, mechanical, nuclear,
% gravitational) and KINETIC energy (radiant, thermal, motion, electrical) —
% but the family split is not part of this table" — a deliberate single-axis
% scope decision, not a missing citation. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? energy_form_family(chemical, $Family)   % potential
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `energy_form_family(form, family)` carrying the table's citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% the family WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains
% on a form this source does not classify.
%
% Discovered via a header-revisit, the same "sibling table, different schema"
% shape already used for `chemistry/chemical-bond-family.adj` (bond →
% family, sibling to `chemical-bonds.adj`) and `comet-part.adj`/
% `comet-tail-type.adj`. WebFetch re-verified the classification live this
% cycle: the SAME EIA "Forms of energy" page (already cited by
% `energy-forms.adj`) opens with "Many forms of energy exist, but energy is
% either potential energy or kinetic energy," then lists Chemical,
% Mechanical, Nuclear, and Gravitational energy under its "Potential energy"
% heading, and Radiant, Thermal, Motion, Sound, and Electrical energy under
% its "Kinetic energy" heading — byte-identical to what `energy-forms.adj`'s
% own header already claimed. (`Sound` energy also appears under Kinetic on
% the source page, but is not one of `energy-forms.adj`'s eight tabled
% forms, so it is out of scope here — same honest-narrowness discipline as
% that table.)
%
% The eight forms and their family, as a little truth table:
%
%     form            family      the page's own grouping
%     -------------   ---------   -------------------------------------
%     chemical        potential   listed under "Potential energy"
%     mechanical      potential   listed under "Potential energy"
%     nuclear         potential   listed under "Potential energy"
%     gravitational   potential   listed under "Potential energy"
%     radiant         kinetic     listed under "Kinetic energy"
%     thermal         kinetic     listed under "Kinetic energy"
%     motion          kinetic     listed under "Kinetic energy"
%     electrical      kinetic     listed under "Kinetic energy"
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a family and recall EVERY form in it (a
% genuine one-to-many reverse recall, four forms per family):
%
%     ? energy_form_family($Form, kinetic)   % radiant ; thermal ; motion ; electrical
%
% A form not one of these eight — sound energy, which the same page also
% names under Kinetic but which `energy-forms.adj` itself never tabled — has
% no row here either, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than inventing a
% family. That honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe to
% reason from.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — the classification was
% read out of the SAME fetched EIA page `energy-forms.adj` already cites, and
% re-verified live this cycle; any form whose family could not be confirmed
% there would have been dropped). All eight rows come from ONE opening
% sentence plus the page's own two-heading structure, EIA "Energy Explained
% — Forms of energy" (eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/forms-of-energy.php),
% quoted here character-for-character:
%
%   "Many forms of energy exist, but energy is either potential energy or
%    kinetic energy."
%
% — followed by the page's own "Potential energy" list (Chemical energy,
% Mechanical energy, Nuclear energy, Gravitational energy) and "Kinetic
% energy" list (Radiant energy, Thermal energy, Motion energy, Sound,
% Electrical energy).
%
% *** THE PAGE DOES NOT STATE ANY ROW'S FAMILY IN A SENTENCE, and that is
% the most important thing to know about this table. *** The classification
% is the page's own STRUCTURE: a "Potential energy" heading with four forms
% listed under it, a "Kinetic energy" heading with the rest. Measured
% 2026-09-14 against the cited page (HTTP 200, 75,012 chars), with the
% envelope sentence found verbatim as a positive control and a fabricated
% sentence reported absent as a negative one: for SEVEN of the eight forms
% there is NO sentence naming both the form and a family. The eighth,
% `motion`, is a false positive — "Kinetic energy is the motion of waves,
% electrons, atoms, molecules, substances, and objects." defines kinetic
% energy and happens to contain the word "motion"; it does not say motion
% energy is kinetic.
%
% SO THIS TABLE IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* CONVERTED TO PER-ROW `source`
% (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, issue #14986), for the same reason as
% `earth-science/water-cycle.adj`: a `source` asserts that the span warrants
% the row, and no span here warrants a family. Membership in a list under a
% heading is also the held "is a list item a verbatim span?" question of
% issue #13934.
%
% WHAT EACH ROW DOES CARRY, added 2026-09-14, is the page's own DEFINITION of
% its form as a row-level `cites` — a corroboration. Before this, seven of
% the eight rows carried no span of their own at all: a query about
% `chemical` came back with only the two-family sentence. It now also
% carries "Chemical energy is energy stored in the bonds of atoms and
% molecules." Each of the eight occurs EXACTLY ONCE on the page, each inside
% a `<p>`, none names another row's form, and — the check that keeps them
% honestly `cites` — none mentions `potential` or `kinetic`.
%
% ONE SPAN IS EXACT AGAINST RENDERED TEXT RATHER THAN AGAINST THE HTML
% BYTES, and the difference is worth one clause: the NUCLEAR sentence's
% em dash is written `&mdash;` in the page source and stored here as
% U+2014. Every other span is pure ASCII and identical either way. So
% "quoted character-for-character" is true of what a reader sees, not of
% the fetched markup.
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, and the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold that single classification sentence — the span that
% fixes the two-family split every row draws from. It names no row key, so it
% mis-warrants nothing, and it is unchanged by this edit. The source is a primary
% U.S. government EIA education page (eia.gov), the same tier
% `energy-forms.adj` already uses, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table energy_form_family {
    columns form, family

    % Each row CITES the page's own definition of its form. `cites`, not
    % `source`: the definition says what chemical energy IS and says
    % nothing about whether it is POTENTIAL or KINETIC. The family comes
    % from which HEADING the form is listed under, and no sentence on the
    % page states it — measured, 7 of the 8 forms have no sentence naming
    % both the form and a family, and the eighth is a false positive.
    %
    % So this table is deliberately NOT converted to per-row `source`
    % (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, issue #14986). A `source` asserts that the span
    % warrants the row; none of these warrants a family.
    row (chemical, potential) {
        cites "Chemical energy is energy stored in the bonds of atoms and molecules." locator "https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/forms-of-energy.php"
    }
    row (mechanical, potential) {
        cites "Mechanical energy is energy stored in objects by tension." locator "https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/forms-of-energy.php"
    }
    row (nuclear, potential) {
        cites "Nuclear energy is energy stored in the nucleus of an atom—the energy that holds the nucleus together." locator "https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/forms-of-energy.php"
    }
    row (gravitational, potential) {
        cites "Gravitational energy is energy stored in an object's height." locator "https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/forms-of-energy.php"
    }
    row (radiant, kinetic) {
        cites "Radiant energy is electromagnetic energy that travels in transverse waves." locator "https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/forms-of-energy.php"
    }
    row (thermal, kinetic) {
        cites "Thermal energy, or heat, is the energy that comes from atoms and molecules moving in a substance." locator "https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/forms-of-energy.php"
    }
    row (motion, kinetic) {
        cites "Motion energy is energy stored in moving objects." locator "https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/forms-of-energy.php"
    }
    row (electrical, kinetic) {
        cites "Electrical energy is delivered by tiny, charged particles, called electrons, that typically move through a wire." locator "https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/forms-of-energy.php"
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE IS UNCHANGED AND IS ALREADY THE RIGHT SPAN: it names
    % no row key and states the two-family split every row draws from.
    % Nothing here mis-warranted a row; the gap this change closes is
    % that seven of the eight rows carried no span of their own at all.
    source "Many forms of energy exist, but energy is either potential energy or kinetic energy."
    locator "https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/forms-of-energy.php"
    trust authoritative
}
